### What was wrong with cap4, precisely

1. **The one-block airfoil-face tip cap is structurally broken.** Two of its four
   sides are chordwise-extended arcs used as index *columns*, squashing 30% of
   chord into one cell width. TFI, Laplacian and Winslow smoothing were all
   measured; every elliptic variant converges to the same −0.02345, which is what
   an elliptic solver doing its job looks like when the boundary is the defect.
2. **The wrap-count law in `MESH_FAMILY_V2.md` was documented but never applied.**
   `wrap_points ~ chord_points · wrap_x/(0.5 − wrap_x)`. Every parameter sweep in
   this study held `wrap_points` fixed while narrowing the wrap band, which forced
   a trade between Jacobian and cell-size range. Obeying the law removes the trade.

### ADR-0006's "not tunable" conclusion rests on a null experiment

ADR-0006 swept `split_x_fore` on `wing_cap4_v1` and read the resulting agreement to
11–12 significant figures as evidence that cap4's tip corner is structural.
**cap4 does not read `split_x_fore`** — `surface.py:776` passes `wrap_x=cap_wrap_x`
for the cap4 path, while `split_x_fore` (line 792) reaches only `mid4`/`split8`. All
three variants were the same mesh; identical numbers were guaranteed by
construction. ADR-0006's other evidence stands (the corner really is ~180°, the
geometry probe is sound, `mid4`/`split8` really are worse); one load-bearing claim
does not. Recorded rather than quietly corrected.

### Two more instrument bugs, found by building S0

**Bug 7 — the verifier crashed on correctly oriented meshes.** `tip_edge_nodes`
took each OML block's last j column as the outboard edge, but
`orient_blocks_consistently` reverses j on every block it flips — seven of eight on
`lhs7_00`. No block then matched the tip station and the check raised `ValueError`
instead of returning a result: a harness crash where a finding belonged. Fixed by
selecting on spanwise position.

**Bug 8 — fidelity reported 835% of local chord on an exact mesh.** The check
decided "not spanwise-refined" from block column COUNT and then assumed column *j*
was station *j*. S0's proportional allocation produces blocks with the right column
count and no column on a station. Two intermediate fixes were also wrong and are
recorded: nearest-station-by-mean-y within half a station gap matched interpolated
columns and reported 10–18%; tightening to node precision matched only 16 of 68 real
station columns, because a block is a *sub-arc* and its mean y is not the whole
contour's. The rule is now the measurement itself — a column is verified when it
lies within the fidelity gate of a station contour. **All 68 station columns then
measure exactly 0.0000000%, at every refinement level.**

That is **eight** measurement bugs found in this study's own instruments.

