**The paper's own progression degrades this geometry monotonically**: ratio 1.00
-> +0.45487, 1.05 -> +0.00508, 1.12 -> -0.05444. Clustering shrinks cells at the
0.0042-chord nose and 0.005-chord base, which are already the tightest features on
a thin-TE BWB section. Defaulted off, knob retained.

**The open problem is inherited from the paper's constants**: nose domain 0.42% of
chord, TE domain 0.125%, so their cells are orders of magnitude finer than
mid-chord. On a rotor blade with a thick blunt TE those domains are proportionally
much larger. Next move is to scale the end stations with local feature size — a
deviation from the paper, to be recorded as one.

