### THE REFINEMENT SET — 30 marches, no epsE passes all ten

    epsE 1.5 -> 5/10      epsE 2.0 -> 6/10      epsE 3.0 -> 7/10

All 30 completed 128 layers. **No value passes every geometry, so ADR-0008 section 6
applies: stop and report, do not extend the ladder.** S1 does not clear the
refinement set, and no epsE is selected.

**The failures split in OPPOSITE directions and the split is predictable.** Three
geometries need MORE dissipation than 1.5; two need LESS than 3.0. The requirement
tracks **min-cell/`s0`** almost monotonically:

| min-cell/`s0` | geometries | requirement |
|---|---|---|
| 6.9 - 9.7 | 001, 004, 000, 006 | need HIGH epsE; **004 fails at all three** |
| 11.7 - 12.7 | 008, 007, 009 | pass at 2.0 and 3.0 |
| 15.2 - 17.6 | 003, 005, 002 | need LOW epsE; 3.0 over-dissipates |

Where the first marching layer is tight against the smallest surface cell the march
needs more smoothing to survive; where it is roomy the same smoothing distorts cells
that were fine. **A single constant cannot serve both ends of that range.**

Union coverage is 9/10 — every geometry except 004 passes at some ladder value — but
picking epsE per geometry by hand is the "no per-geometry manual repair" gate
(ADR-0011 section 6.1 item 11). An automated **law** epsE(min/`s0`) would not be
manual repair and the correlation is strong enough to fit one, but that deviates
from ADR-0008's fixed ladder and needs an ADR first.

### Instrument bug 10 — the checklist scored an INCOMPLETE march as PASS

`lhs100_seed42_009` at epsE 3.0 was still running when first collected: 95 of 128
layers, no bad layer *yet*, every other condition satisfied — **PASS**. A march that
has not finished has not passed anything. `volume_gate_checklist` now requires
`march_completed`. Found because the layer count in the table looked wrong, not
because anything flagged it.

**Also fixed: `prepare` generated commands with no output redirect**, so pyHyp wrote
a CGNS and no `run_stdout.log` — and `collect` parses that log whenever
`volume_report.json` is absent, which is always for the static runner. The first
30-march campaign was unreadable and had to be re-run. That defect would have
silently wasted every future campaign, S2-S5 included.

**The epsE ordering is the OPPOSITE of everything else measured here.** S0 needed
the ladder floor (1.5 best; 3.0 invalid); Stage 02's S1 at `fine` also preferred
less dissipation. This S1 wants the ceiling. Per-strategy epsE (ADR-0011 section 5)
is vindicated by exactly this. **Risk:** 3.0 is the TOP of the declared ladder, so a
geometry needing more has nowhere to go, and ADR-0008 section 6 forbids extending it
without an ADR.

**S1's surface gate still fails one item** — fidelity unverified for the
spanwise-refined blocks, with all 102 station columns at 0.0000000%. Same
cross-cutting item as section 0.6(b). S1 cannot drop the refinement: it is what made
the volume valid.

Against S0's frozen best of 35/128 at -0.11063: **S1 has thirty-five times fewer bad
layers at comparable minimum quality.** It is the best volume result in this study.

**What fixed it was not the cell-size range.** Step 1->2 cut the range from 810 to
173 and the march still exploded at layer 2 — the range is necessary but not
sufficient, which is a correction to how ADR-0010 has been applied here. The cause
was an **interface spacing jump**: outboard spanwise cells of 14-21 mm against
tip-cap radial cells of 0.13-1.0 mm, 28:1 across the tip interface. RUNBOOK section 7
Round A already lists "interface spacing jump" as a reported metric; nothing in this
study had been measuring it.

**The fix is the equation Openblademesh does not implement.** Section 3.1.5 gives
eq 3.5-3.10 for one geometric series over the whole wing, states the requirement —
"the cells on the wing need to be very small... as similar as possible to the cells
on the wingtip on the edge" — and then says the formula "is given here but is not
used" because the algebraic route "takes too long" and the numerical one "is yet to
be finished". Implementing it by walking rather than solving took S1 from divergence
to a valid volume.

So: following the paper faithfully reproduced the paper's own reported failure
(section 3.1.7, "Pyhyp cannot extrude the whole wing"), and fixing it required
supplying the part the paper leaves undone.

### DECISION-0004 VIOLATED, THEN VALIDATED (2026-08-14)

**The study was meshing the wrong trailing edge.** Both S0 and S1 used
`te_thickness = 0.005` as a chord **FRACTION**. Measured absolute edge:

| geometry | root chord | root TE | tip chord | tip TE |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| lhs100_seed42_003 | 945.8 mm | **4.73 mm** | 178.5 mm | 0.89 mm |
| lhs100_seed42_004 | 852.9 mm | **4.26 mm** | 93.4 mm | 0.47 mm |
| lhs100_seed42_001 | 992.9 mm | **4.96 mm** | 91.6 mm | 0.46 mm |

DECISION-0004 decides the opposite: "As-built aircraft TE: a **FIXED (constant along
span) absolute thickness = 0.5 mm**", chosen explicitly for manufacturability over a
fractional law, and "Mesh: model the AS-BUILT 0.5 mm TE." So the study meshed a
root edge **~9x too thick** and a spanwise-VARYING edge — the exact option the
decision rejected. Both strategies were consistently wrong, not inconsistent.

**It was also the root cause of failures chased for two sessions.** Because the
fraction shrinks with chord, the smallest surface cell sits at the TIP trailing edge
and scales with tip chord. That is why S0's `thin_small_tip` extreme failed
(min-cell/`s0` 5.5), why S1's geometry 004 failed at all three epsE, and why
min-cell/`s0` varied 6.9 to 17.6 across ten geometries so that no single epsE could
serve both ends.

Fixed in `shared/ingestion.py` — the as-built edge is aircraft geometry, not a
per-strategy meshing choice, so it now applies identically to S0, S1 and every later
strategy. S1's min-cell/`s0` spread collapsed **2.55x -> 1.29x**.

### ...and the constant 0.5 mm edge is NOT marchable inboard

| epsE | fractional TE (wrong) | constant 0.5 mm (correct aircraft) |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 1.5 | 5/10 | **0/10** |
| 2.0 | 6/10 | **1/10** |
| 3.0 | 7/10 | **4/10** |

The manufacturable edge is *harder* to mesh, because 0.5 mm is only ~0.05%c at the
root. **DECISION-0004 anticipated exactly this and authorised the remedy:**

> "If a real pyHyp march cannot advance the thin inboard TE (0.5 mm ~ 0.05%c at
> root), apply a **mesh-only local inboard floor (~0.25%c)** — a NUMERICAL artifact
> of the solver, not a change to the aircraft, and quantified here as ~0 drag. ...
> **Pending pyHyp validation (see Open items).**"

**This study has now produced that validation**, which DECISION-0004 lists as an open
item: the pure constant edge does not march, and the floor is required. Implemented
as `TE_MESH_FLOOR_FRAC = 0.0025`, i.e. `max(0.25%c, 0.5 mm)` — root 2.1 mm, tip
0.5 mm. **The aircraft is unchanged**: constant 0.5 mm is still what goes to the shop.

**S0 note:** the corrected edge also breaks S0's metric invariance — min scaled
Jacobian is now geometry-dependent (0.140-0.196) instead of a constant +0.19113,
which is more informative. But S0's min-cell/`s0` spread stays **2.16x**, because its
smallest cell is in `tip_collar_te`, sized by `wrap_x x tip_chord` — still a chord
fraction by construction. The TE fix helps S1 structurally and only partly helps S0.

**All S0 results recorded before this fix are on the wrong aircraft and must be
re-run**, including the `thin_small_tip` extreme failure.

