# 5H. epsE CONFIRMED, AND A RESTRUCTURE AGREED (2026-08-14)

## 5H.1 Ladder at the finer level `fine` (N=193, coarsen=1) — all ten geometries

| epsE | passing all ten @ fine |
|---:|---:|
| **1.5** | **10/10** |
| 2.0 | 9/10 (lhs7_01 fails, minQ -0.210) |
| 3.0 | 6/10 |

At 1.5 every geometry gives 0 bad layers, min quality +0.103 to +0.250, all
positive. Ordering is monotonic: **less dissipation is uniformly better here**,
the opposite of the usual expectation.

`lhs7_01` was not a broken geometry - it has a narrower stable epsE window and
passes at 1.5 (+0.204).

**This was the first fully protocol-compliant result in the study:** calibrated on
ten geometries, confirmed at a genuinely finer level, selected by a rule written
before any result was seen. cap4 baseline: 53/128 bad at -0.908.

`epsE_common_start = 2.0` from the earlier calibration is **superseded** - it
failed finer-level confirmation. S1's own value is **1.5**.

## 5H.2 RESTRUCTURE AGREED — six independent studies

Stage 02 compared one topology wearing four labels: the tip closure was built once
and inherited, so S3/S4/S5 converged on S1's cap. S4's own analytic tip was built,
measured, rejected, and replaced by S1's.

**The full restructure brief is `AERIS_MESH_STUDY/RESTRUCTURE_PROMPT.md`.**
Headlines:

- six independent studies, each implemented from scratch in
  `04_strategy_studies/S<N>_<name>/`
- a hard **independence line**: all blocking, tip closure, spanwise and chordwise
  laws are per-strategy; only CAD ingestion, QC *definitions*, gate thresholds,
  the pyHyp invocation, geometry sets and the verifier are shared
- **`COMMON_BRIEF.md`** gives every strategy Stage 02's lessons up front, so the
  last-built does not win on hindsight
- **epsE is per-strategy**, not global - a value calibrated on S1's cell
  distribution would rig the comparison
- metrics and ranking **frozen before any strategy is optimised**
- **robustness measured on the HOLD-OUT**, never the refinement set, or the
  top-ranked criterion becomes a proxy for effort
- an explicit **no-winner rule** if nothing passes all hard gates
- a **per-strategy effort ledger** in `status`, reported with the results
- ADR-0011 supersedes **ADR-0006** (S0 is now developed to its best state, not
  recorded as failed-not-repaired)

## 5H.3 Migration hazard, recorded

`stage02_common.py` is **not** a clean shared module - S1's blocking decisions are
baked in as defaults (`feature_split_sides(distribution="uniform")`,
`butterfly_from_ring` width/inset/collar, `realise_spanwise_law`,
`oml_tip_ring_2d`). Migrating it unchanged would hand every strategy S1's answers
and recreate the exact problem the restructure exists to fix. §8 of the brief
lists what moves to S1's folder and what stays shared with defaults stripped.

## 5H.4 Objective restated

The deliverable is **one scalable, robust method that runs unattended across the
DSE** - not a well-documented catalogue of failures. Stage 02 was rigorous about
recording what did not work and slow to produce something that did. If a strategy
is close to passing, finish it.

