# 5I. S4 COMPLETE — THE CONSTRUCTED VOLUME (2026-08-16)

## 5I.1 Governance first: ADR-0014

ADR-0011 §6.1's volume gate is phrased in terms of pyHyp's report, and RUNBOOK §6 S4
exempts S4 from pyHyp. **ADR-0014 was written before any S4 volume existed**, restating
the same five conditions in terms of the mesh rather than the tool, with every ADR-0008
threshold unchanged. `shared/volume_qc.py` supplies them; `gates.volume_gate_checklist_direct`
sits beside the pyHyp checklist rather than replacing it.

**ADR-0014 §3.4 equivalence: VERIFIED**, against 6 completed S1 marches.

- validity agreement: exact (0 inverted, min volume > 0 on both routes)
- sign agreement: exact
- **quality ratio in-house / pyHyp: 0.585 to 0.660** — a stable factor, recorded
  because the two are different definitions of the same idea and the final report must
  never mix them. S1's volumes read 0.193–0.364 through pyHyp and **0.170–0.231**
  through the in-house metric.

The check earned its place immediately: the first hexahedral scaled Jacobian ordered
its corner triple product so "positive" meant the opposite of what `signed_cell_volumes`
means by it. It passed a unit-cube test and scored every valid S1 volume at −1.0.

## 5I.2 The result

| | S4 | S1, same geometries and level |
|---|---:|---:|
| surface built / watertight | **10/10** | 10/10 |
| deterministic connectivity | True, 1 signature | True |
| worst surface min scaled Jacobian | **+0.01054** | +0.236 |
| surface cell-size range | 198 – 426 | 114 |
| **OML volume min scaled quality** | **+0.167 … +0.951** | — |
| **OML volume mean scaled quality** | **0.92 … 0.98** | — |
| OML volume inverted cells | **0** | — |
| tip-cap volume | −0.71 … −0.81, 54–155 inverted | — |
| volume gate | **FAIL 0/10** | PASS 10/10 (pyHyp route) |
| in-house min scaled quality, whole mesh | negative (tip cap) | **0.170 – 0.231** |

**Over the wing itself a constructed volume beats a marched one, and not narrowly.**
S4's OML blocks mean 0.92–0.98 against S1's whole-mesh minimum of 0.170–0.231, and 96–98%
of S4's cells sit at or above the 0.30 ranking target. S4 loses on the tip cap alone.

Cell counts are **not** comparable — S4 builds the boundary layer, pyHyp marches to a
far field (ADR-0014 §4).

## 5I.3 The finding: the exchange rate between base count and corner sharpness

ADR-0013 §2 said an O-H ring forces the nose wrap and the 1.0 mm base to equal counts,
and that there are "essentially two ways to satisfy it", S0's and S1's. **There is a
third**, and the paper states it — §2.3.1: *"a single boundary edge may consist of
multiple control edges"*. The equality is between boundary edges, not arcs. S4's ring
achieves perfectly balanced boundary edges (**23/22/23/22**) with the base at **3 points,
chosen on physical grounds**.

**And it has a price, now quantified.** The section has exactly two sharp corners, the
two blunt-TE base corners, and putting a ring corner on both isolates the base again.
So at most one is usable and the other three ring corners come from smooth contour.
Interior angles at the eight Type 1 vertices, identical on every geometry:

    v0 114.0°  v1 179.0°  v2 178.2°  v3 176.3°  v4 176.8°  v5 179.3°  v6 180.9°  v7 73.2°

- **v6 is reflex.** Every ring containing it folds exactly one core cell at −0.01523;
  every ring without it is positive. Exhaustive search, 22 admissible rings built.
- **The cap's min scaled Jacobian is `sin(180° − flattest ring corner)`.** Selected ring
  (1,3,5,7), flattest corner v5 at 179.31°, sin(0.69°) = 0.0120. **Measured 0.01198.**
- It is **flat under refinement** — 0.0124 at L1 through L5, a 16x cell-count increase —
  because it is set by an angle.

**S0 and S1 keep the sharp corners and pay in cell-size range. S4 frees the base count
and pays at the corners. Two ends of one constraint, and this is the first time the
study can write down the exchange rate.**

## 5I.4 Fourth confirmation of the central result, and a sharpening of it

S4 uses a uniform spanwise law with **no tip anchoring** — deliberately, since anchoring
is S1's fix for a marching problem S4 does not have. Its OML volume blocks are
nonetheless excellent (0.92–0.98 mean, zero inverted).

**So the tip-interface match is a property of hyperbolic marching, not of the mesh.**
S1 needs it, S3 died without it, S4 does not need it because nothing marches through the
interface.

