# 5J. THE FIDELITY GATE IS GENERATOR-CHANGING, NOT MEASUREMENT-ONLY (2026-08-16)

**Question:** the fidelity condition fails for every strategy ("fidelity unverified for
spanwise-refined blocks"). Can `shared/exact_sections.py` be used to *verify* those
columns where they are, or does the generator have to change? The two have very
different prices — the first is an afternoon and re-runs nothing, the second
re-identifies all three locked geometry sets and re-runs everything.

**Answer: the generator has to change.** Settled on the measurements already in
`shared/exact_sections.py`, no new runs needed.

A measurement-only fix would require the interpolated columns to be *on* the loft and
merely unproven. They are not on it. On `lhs100_seed42_000` the wing departs from a
straight line between generator stations by a **median 0.447% of chord and up to 7.25%
at the inboard/outboard spline junction — 45x to 700x the 0.01% gate.** Those columns
are genuinely off the aircraft; no measurement makes them pass, and one that did would
be an instrument bug of exactly the kind COMMON_BRIEF section 5 exists to prevent.

Refining the station count does not rescue it either: 17 -> 35 -> 71 -> 143 stations
gives 7.25% -> 3.08% -> 1.42% -> 0.68%, a ratio of ~2 per doubling. That is FIRST-order
convergence — linear interpolation across a slope discontinuity — so reaching 0.01%
needs of order 12,000 stations.

**So the fix is the one `exact_sections.py` was written for:** the mesher asks the
generator for sections at exactly the stations it wants, every mesh column is a real
section, and the deviation is identically zero by construction on every geometry.
`planform_curves()` already reproduces the generator to **0.000e+00** and `exact_z_at()`
replaces the trapezoid integration of `tan(dihedral)` with its closed form, which is
what makes a section at arbitrary `y` well defined at all.

**Consequences, which are why this is an ADR and not a commit:**

- it modifies `src/aeris` generator code, untouched by this study so far;
- it changes what `configs/geometry/bwb.yaml` means, hence the sha256 identifying
  `epse_calibration_lhs10_seed7`, `lhs100_seed42` and `round_c_lhs10_seed42`;
- **every surface and volume result in this study is measured on the old sets** and
  would have to be re-run, S1's 10/10 marches included;
- the hold-out must be re-identified before it is used, or it is no longer the set the
  study froze.

**Recommendation: do it, and do it before the hold-out run**, not after. Running the
hold-out on sets that are about to be re-identified spends the one-shot budget on a
geometry set the final report will not be using.

